t, and a regular value y 2 N , we define ]f 1 .y/ to be the number of points in f 1 .y/. The first observation to be made about ]f 1 .y/ is that it is locally constant as a function of y (where y ranges only through regular values!). I.e., there is a neighborhood V  N of y such that ]f 1 .y 0 / D ]f 1 .y/ for any y 0 2 V . [Let x1 ;    ; xk be the points of f 1 .y/, and choose pairwise disjoint neighborhoods U1 ;    ; Uk of these which are mapped diffeomorphically onto neighborhoods V1 ;    ; Vk in N . We may then take V D V1 \ V2 \    \ Vk<br /> <br /> f .M<br /> <br /> U1<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Uk /:<br /> <br /> The fundamental theorem of algebra As an application of these notions, we prove the fundamental theorem of algebra: every nonconstant complex polynomial P .z/ must have a zero. For the proof it is first necessary to pass from the plane of complex numbers to a compact manifold. Consider the unit sphere S2  R3 and the stereographic projection hC W S2 ¹.0; 0; 1/º ! R2  0  R3<br /> <br /> from the “north pole” .0; 0; 1/ of S2 . (See Figure 3.) We will identify R2  0 with the plane of complex numbers. The polynomial map P from R2  0 to itself corresponds to a map f from S2 to itself; where f .x/ D hC1 P hC .x/ for x ¤ .0; 0; 1/<br /> <br /> f .0; 0; 1/ D .0; 0; 1/:<br /> <br /> It is well known that this resulting map f is smooth, even in a neighborhood of the north pole. To see this we introduce the stereographic projection h from the south pole .0; 0; 1/ and set Q.z/ D h f h<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .z/:<br /> <br /> The fundamental theorem of algebra<br /> <br /> 9<br /> <br /> Note, by elementary geometry, that hC h<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> Now if P .z/ D a0 z n C a1 z n computation shows that Q.z/ D<br /> <br /> .z/ D 1<br /> <br /> z D 1=Nz: jzj2<br /> <br /> C    C an , with a0 ¤ 0, then a short<br /> <br /> zn : aN 0 C aN 1 z C    C aN n z n<br /> <br /> Thus Q is smooth in a neighborhood of 0, and it follows that f D h smooth in a neighborhood of .0; 0; 1/.<br /> <br /> 1 Qh<br /> <br /> is<br /> <br /> .0; 0; 1/ x<br /> <br /> hC .x/<br /> <br /> R2  0 S2<br /> <br /> Figure 3: Stereographic projection Next observe that f has only a finite number of critical pointsl for P fails to be a local only at the zeros of the derivative polynomial P diffeomorphism 0 j 1 P .z/ D an j jz , and there are only finitely many zeros since P 0 is not identically zero. The set of regular values of f , being a sphere with finitely many points removed, is therefore connected. Hence the locally constant function ]f 1 .y/ must actually be constant on this set. Since ]f 1 .y/ can’t be zero everywhere, we conclude that it is zero nowhere. Thus f is an onto mapping, and the polynomial P must have a zero.<br /> <br /> 10<br /> <br /> 2 The theorem of Sard and Brown In general, it is too much to hope that the set of critical values of a smooth map to be finite. But this set will be “small”, in the sense indicated by the next theorem, which was proved by A. Sard in 1942 following earlier work by A. P. Morse. (References [30], [24].) Theorem. Let f W U ! Rn be a smooth map, defined on an open set U  Rm , and let C D ¹ x 2 U j rank dfx < n º: Then the image f .C /  Rn has Lebesgue measure zero.1<br /> <br /> Since a set of measure zero cannot contain any nonvacuous open set, it follows that the complement Rn f .C / must be everywhere dense2 The proof will be given in §3. It is essential for the proof that f should have many derivatives. (Compare Whitney [38].) We will be mainly interested in the case m  n. If m < n, then clearly C D U ; hence the theorem says simply that f .U / has measure zero. More generally consider a smooth map f W M ! N , from a manifold of dimension m to a manifold of dimension n. Let C be a set of all x 2 M such that dfx W TMx ! T Nf .x/ has rank less than n (i.e. is not onto). Then C will be called the set of critical points, f .C / the set of critical values, and the complement N f .C / the set of regular values of f . (This agrees with our previous definitions in the case m D n.) Since M can be covered by a countable collection of neighborhoods each diffeomorphic to an open subset of Rm , we have Corollary (A. B. Brown). The set of regular values of a smooth map f W M ! N is everywhere dense in N . 1 In other words,<br /> <br /> given any " > 0, it is possible to cover f .C / by a sequence of cubes in Rn having total n-dimensional volume less than ". 2 Proved by Arthur B. Brown in 1935. This result was rediscovered by Dubovickiǐ in 1953 and by Thom in 1954. (References [5], [8], [36].)<br /> <br /> 11 In order to exploit this corollary we will need the following: Lemma 1. If f W M ! N is a smooth map between manifolds of dimension m  n, and if y 2 N is a regular value, then the set f 1 .y/  M is a smooth manifold of dimension m n. Proof. Let x 2 f 1 .y/. Since y is a regular value, the derivative dfx must map TMx onto T Ny . The null space N  TMx of dfx will therefore be an .m n/-dimensional vector space. If M  Rk , choose a linear map LW Rk ! Rm n that is nonsingular on this subspace N  TMx  Rk . Now define F W M ! N  Rm<br /> <br /> n<br /> <br /> by F ./ D .f ./; L.//. The derivative dFx is clearly given by the formula dFx .v/ D .dfx .v/; L.v//: Thus dFx is nonsingular. Hence F maps some neighborhood U of x diffeomorphically onto a neighborhood V of .y; L.x//. Note that f 1 .y/ corresponds, under F , to the hyperplane y Rm n \V . This proves that f 1 .y/ is a smooth manifold of dimension m n. ■ As an example we can give an easy proof that the unit sphere Sm smooth manifold. Consider the function f W Rm ! R defined by<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> is a<br /> <br /> 2 f .x/ D x12 C x22 C    C xm :<br /> <br /> Any y ¤ 0 is a regular value, and the smooth manifold f 1 .1/ is the unit sphere. If M 0 is a manifold which is contained in M , it has already been noted that TMx0 is a subspace of TMx for x 2 M 0 . The orthogonal 