 all x 2 Sn ;<br /> <br /> (1)<br /> <br /> The Brouwer degree<br /> <br /> 31<br /> <br /> using the Euclidean inner product. If v.x/ is nonzero for all x, then we may as well suppose that v.x/  v.x/ D 1<br /> <br /> for all x 2 Sn :<br /> <br /> (2)<br /> <br /> v.x/ For in any case v.x/ N D kv.x/k would be a vector field which does satisfy this condition. Thus we can think of v as a smooth function from Sn to itself. Now define a smooth homotopy<br /> <br /> F W Sn  Œ0;  ! Sn by the formula F .x;  / D x cos  C v.x/ sin . Computation shows that F .x;  /  F .x;  / D 1 and that F .x; 0/ D x;<br /> <br /> F .x; / D<br /> <br /> x:<br /> <br /> Thus the antipodal map of Sn is homotopic to the identity. But for n even we have seen that this is impossible. On the other hand, if n D 2k 1, the explicit formula v.x1 ;    ; x2k / D .x2<br /> <br /> x1 ; x4<br /> <br /> x3 ; : : : ; x2k<br /> <br /> x2k<br /> <br /> 1/<br /> <br /> defines a nonzero tangent vector field on Sn . This completes the proof. It follows, incidentally, that the antipodal map of Sn is homotopic to the identity for n odd. A famous theorem due to Heinz Hopf asserts that two mappings from a connected n-manifold to the n-sphere are smoothly homotopic if and only if they have the same degree. In §7 we will prove a more general result which implies Hopf’s theorem.<br /> <br /> 32<br /> <br /> 6 Vector fields and the Euler number As a further application of the concept of degree, we study vector fields on other manifolds. Consider first an open set U  Rm and a smooth vector field vW U ! Rm with an isolated zero at the point z 2 U . The function N D v.x/<br /> <br /> v.x/ kv.x/k<br /> <br /> maps a small sphere centered at z into the unit sphere1 . The degree of this mapping is called the index  of v at the zero z. Some examples, with indices 1; 0; 1; 2, are illustrated in Figure 12. (Intimately associated with v are the curves “tangent” to v which are obtained i by solving the differetial equations dx dt D vi .x1 ;    ; xn /. It is these curves which are actually sketched in Figure 12.) A zero with arbitrary index can be obtained as follows: In the plane of complex numbers the polynomial z k defines a smooth vector field with zero of index k at the origin, and the function zN k defines a vector field with a zero of index k. We must prove that this concept of index is invariant under diffeomorphism of U . To explain what this means, let us consider the more general situation of a map f W M ! N , with a vector field on each manifold. Definition. The vector fields v on M and v 0 on N correspond under f if the derivative dfx carries v.x/ into v 0 .f .x// for each x 2 M . If f is a diffeomorphism, then clearly v 0 is uniquely determined by v. The notation v 0 D df ı v ı f 1 will be used. 1 Each<br /> <br /> sphere is to be oriented as the boundary of the corresponding disk.<br /> <br /> Euler number<br /> <br /> D<br /> <br /> 33<br /> <br /> D<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br />  D C1<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br />  D C2<br /> <br /> Figure 12: Examples of plane vector fields. Lemma 1. Suppose that the vector field v on U corresponds to v 0 D df ı v ı f<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> on U 0 under a diffeomorphism f W U ! U 0 . Then the index of v at an isolated zero z is equal to the index of v 0 at f .z/. Assuming Lemma 1, we can define the concept of index for a vector field w on an arbitrary manifold M as follows: If gW U ! M is a parametrization of a neighborhood of z in M , then the index  of w at z is defined to be equal to the index of the corresponding vector field dg 1 ı w ı g on U at the zero g 1 .z/. It clearly will follow from Lemma 1 that  is well defined. The proof of Lemma 1 will be bases on the proof of a quite different result: Lemma 2. Any orientation preserving diffeomorphism f of Rm is smoothly isotopic to the identity. (In contrast, for many values of m there exists an orientation preserving<br /> <br /> 34<br /> <br /> 6. Vecter fields<br /> <br /> diffeomorphism of the sphere Sm which is not smoothly isotopic to the identity. See [20, p. 404].) Proof. defined by<br /> <br /> We may assume that f .0/ D 0. Since the derivative at 0 can be f .tx/ ; t !0 t<br /> <br /> df0 .x/ D lim it is natural to define an isotopy<br /> <br /> F W Rm  Œ0; 1 ! Rm by the formula f .tx/ for 0 < t  1; t F .x; 0/ D df0 .x/: F .x; t/ D<br /> <br /> To prove that F is smooth, even as t ! 0, we write f in the form2 f .x/ D x1 g1 .x/ C    C xm gm .x/; where g1 ;    ; gm are suitable smooth functions, and note that F .x; t/ D x1 g1 .tx/ C    C xm gm .tx/ for all values of t. Thus f is isotopic to the linear mapping df0 , which is clearly isotopic to the identity. This proves Lemma 2. ■ Proof of Lemma 1. We may assume that z D f .z/ D 0 and that U is convex. If f preserves orientation, then, proceeding exactly as above, we construct a one-parameter family of embeddings ft W U ! Rm with f0 D identity, f1 D f , and ft .0/ D 0 for all t. Let v, denote the vector field dft ı v ı ft 1 on ft .U /, which corresponds to v on U . These vector fields are all defined and nonzero on a suﬀiciently small sphere centered at 0. Hence the index of v D v0 at 0 must be equal to the index of v 0 D v1 at 0. This proves Lemma 1 for orientation preserving diffeomorphisms. To consider diffeomorphisms which reverse orientation it is suﬀicient to 2 See<br /> <br /> for example [22, p. 5].<br /> <br /> Euler number<br /> <br /> 35<br /> <br /> consider the special case of a reflection . Then v0 D  ı v ı  so the associated function vN 0 .x/ D<br /> <br /> v 0 .x/ kv 0 .x/k<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> ;<br /> <br /> on the "-sphere satisfies<br /> <br /> vN 0 D  ı vN ı <br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> :<br /> <br /> Evidently the degree of vN 0 equals the degree of v, N which completes the proof ■ of Lemma 1. We will study the following classical result: Let M be a compact manifold and w a smooth vector field on M with isolated zeros. If M has a boundary, then w is required to point outward at all boundary points. P Poincaré-Hopf Theorem. The sum  of the indices at the zeros of such a vector field is equal to the Euler number3 m X .M / D . 1/i rank Hi .M /: i D0<br /> <br /> In particular this index sum is a topological invariant of M : it does not depend on the particular choice of vector field. (A 2-dimensional version of this theorem was proved by Poincaré in 1885. The full theorem was proved by Hopf [14] in 1926 after earlier partial results by Brouwer and Hadamard.) We will prove part of this theorem, and sketch a proof of the rest. First consider the special case of a compact domain in Rm . Let X  Rm be a compact m-manifold with boundary. The Gauss mapping gW @X ! Sm<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> assigns to each x 2 @X the outward unit normal vector at x. Lemma 3 (Hopf). If vW X ! Rm is a smooth vector field with isolated P zeros, and if v points out of X along the boundary, then the index sum P  is equal to the degree of the Gauss mapping from @X to Sm 1 . In particular,  does not depend on the choice of v. For example, if a vector field on the disk Dm points outward along the 3 Here H .M / denotes the i-th homology group of M . This will be our first and last i reference to homology theory.<br /> <br /> 36<br /> <br /> 6. Vecter fields<br /> <br /> Figure 13: An example with index sum C1. boundary, the<br /> <br /> P<br /> <br />  D C1. (Compare Figure 13.)<br /> <br /> Proof. Removing an "-ball aroung each zero, we obtain a new manifold v.x/ with boundary. The function v.x/ N D kv.x/k maps this manifold into Sm 1 . Hence the sum of the degrees of vN restricted to the various boundary components is zero. But vj N @X is homotopic to g, and the degrees on the other boundary P components add up to . (The minus sign occurs since each small sphere gets the wrong orientation.) Therefore X deg.g/ D0 as required.<br /> <br /> ■<br /> <br /> Remark. The degree of g is also known as the “curvatura integra” of @X, since it can be expressed as a constant times the integral over @X of the Gaussian curvature. This integer is of course equal to the Euler number of X . For m odd it is equal to half the Euler number of @X. Before extending this result to other manifolds, some more preliminaries are needed. It is natural to try to compute the index of a vector field v at a zero z in terms of the derivatives of v at z. Consider first a vector field v on an open set U  Rm and think of v as a mapping U ! Rm , so that dvx W Rm ! Rm is defined. Definition. The vector field v is nondegenerate at z if the linear transformation dvx is nonsingular. It follows that z is and isolated zero. Lemma 4. The index of v at nondegenerate zero z is either C1 or according as the determinant of dvx is positive or negative.<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> Euler number<br /> <br /> 37<br /> <br /> Proof. Think of v as a diffeomorphism from some convex neighborhood U0 of z into Rm . We may assume that z D 0. If v preserves orientation, we have seen that vjU0 can be deformed smoothly into the identity without introducing any new zeros. (See Lemmas 1, 2.) Hence the index is certainly equal to C1. If v reverses orientation, the similarly v can be deformed into a reflection; hence  D 1. ■ More generally consider a zero z of a vector field w on a manifold M  Rk . Think of w as a map from M to Rk so that the derivative dwz W TMz ! Rk is defined. Lemma 5. The derivative dwz actually carries TMz into the subspace TMz  Rk , and hence can be consider as a linear transformation from TMz to itself. If this linear transformation has determinant D ¤ 0 then z is an isolated zero of w with index equal to C1 or 1 according as D is positive or negative. Proof. Let hW U ! M be a parametrization of some neighborhood of z. Let e i denote the i -th basis vector of Rm and let t i D dhu .e i / D<br /> <br /> @h @ui<br /> <br /> so that the vectors t 1 ;    ; t m form a basis for that tangent space TMh.u/ . We mus