ere exists a vector field on M with no zeros at all. In order to obtain the full strength of the Poincaré-Hopf theorem, three 5 For<br /> <br /> example, v can be defined by the formula v.x/ D p pole. (See Figure 11.)<br /> <br /> .p  x/x, where p is the north<br /> <br /> 40<br /> <br /> 6. Vecter fields<br /> <br /> further steps are needed. P Step 1. Identification of the invariant  with the Euler number .M /. It is suﬀicient to construct just one example of a nondegenerate vector field on M P with  equal to .M /. The most pleasant way of doing this is the following: According to Marston Morse, it is always possible to find a real valued function on M whose “gradient” is a nondegenerate vector field. Furthermore, Morse showed that the sum of indices associated with such a gradient field is equal to the Euler number of M . For details of this argument the reader is referred to Milnor [22, pp. 29-36]. Step 2. Proving the theorem for a vector field with degenerate zeros. Consider first a vector field v on an open set U with an isolated zero at z. If W U ! Œ0; 1 takes the value 1 on a small neighborhood N1 of z and the value 0 outside a slightly larger neighborhood N , and if y is suﬀiciently small regular value of v, then the vector field v 0 .x/ D v.x/ .x/y<br /> <br /> in nondegenerate6 within N . The sum of the indices at the zeros within N can be evaluated as the degree of the map vW N @N ! Sm<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> ;<br /> <br /> and hence does not change during this alteration. More generally consider vector fields on a compact manifold M . Applying this argument locally we see that any vector field with isolated zeros Pcan be replaced by a nondegenerate vector field without altering the integer . Step 3. Manifolds with boundary. If M  Rk has a boundary, then any vector field v which points outward along @M can again be extended over the neighborhood N" so as to point outward along @N" . However, there is some diﬀiculty with smoothness around the boundary of M . Thus N" is not a smooth (i.e. differentiable of class C 1 ) manifold, but only a C 1 -manifold. The extension w, if defined as before by w.x/ D v.r.x// C x r.x/, will only be a continuous vector field near @M . The argument can nonetheless be carried out either by showing that our strong differentiability assumptions are not really necessary or by other methods.<br /> <br /> 6 Clearly<br /> <br /> v 0 is nondegenerate within N1 . But if y is suﬀiciently small, then v 0 will have no zeros at all within N N1 .<br /> <br /> 41<br /> <br /> 7 Framed cobordism The Pontryagin construction The degree of a mapping M ! M 0 is defined only when the manifolds M and M 0 are oriented and have the same dimension. We will study a generalization, due to Pontryagin, which is defined for a smooth map f W M ! Sp from an arbitrary compact, boundaryless manifold to a sphere. First some definitions. Let N and N 0 be compact n-dimensional submanifolds of M with @N D @N 0 D @M D ∅. The difference of dimensions m n is called the codimension of the submanifolds. Definition. N is cobordant to N 0 within M if the subset N  Œ0; "/ [ N 0  .1<br /> <br /> "; 1<br /> <br /> of M  Œ0; 1 can be extended to a compact manifold X  M  Œ0; 1 so that @X D N  0 [ N 0  1; and so that X does not intersect M  0 [ M  1 except at the points of @X. Clearly cobordism is an equivalence relation. (See Figure 15.) Definition. A framing of a submanifold N  M is a smooth function v which assigns to each x 2 N a basis v.x/ D .v 1 .x/;    ; v m<br /> <br /> n<br /> <br /> .x//<br /> <br /> for the space T Nx?  TMx of normal vectors to N in M at x. (See Figure 16.) The pair .N; v/ is called a framed submanifold of M . Two framed submanifolds .N; v/ and .N 0 ; w/ are framed cobordant if there exists a cobordism X  M <br /> <br /> 42<br /> <br /> 7. Framed cobordism<br /> <br /> M 0<br /> <br /> M 1<br /> <br /> M 2<br /> <br /> Figure 15: Pasting together two cobordisms within M . Œ0; 1 between N and N 0 and a framing u of X, so that ui .x; t/ D .v i .x/; 0/<br /> <br /> ui .x; t/ D .w i .x/; 0/<br /> <br /> for .x; t / 2 N  Œ0; "/; for<br /> <br /> .x; t / 2 N 0  .1<br /> <br /> "; 1:<br /> <br /> Again this is an equivalence relation. Now consider a smooth map f W M ! Sp and a regular value y 2 Sp . The map f induces a framing of the manifold f 1 .y/ as follows: Choose a positively oriented basis v D .v1 ;    ; vp / for the tangent space T .Sp /y . For each x 2 f 1 .y/ recall from page 11 that dfx W TMx ! T .Sp /y maps the subspace Tf 1 .y/x to zero and maps its orthogonal complement p Tf 1 .y/? x isomorphically onto T .S /y . Hence there is a unique vector w i .x/ 2 Tf<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .y/? x  TMx<br /> <br /> that maps into v i under dfx . It will be convenient to use the notation w D f  v for the resulting framing w 1 .x/;    ; w p .x/ of f 1 .y/. Definition. This framed manifold .f tryagin manifold associated with f .<br /> <br /> 1 .y/; f  v/<br /> <br /> will be called the Pon-<br /> <br /> Of course f has many Pontryagin manifolds, corresponding to different choices of y and v, but they all belong to a single framed cobordism class: Theorem A. If y 0 is another regular value of f and v0 is a positively oriented basis for T .Sp /y 0 , then the framed manifold .f 1 .y 0 /; f  v0 / is framed cobordant to .f 1 .y/; f  v/. Theorem B. Two mappings from M to Sp are smoothly homotopic if and only if the associated Pontryagin manifolds are framed cobordant.<br /> <br /> The Pontryagin construction<br /> <br /> 43<br /> <br /> M N<br /> <br /> N<br /> <br /> X<br /> <br /> M  Œ0; 1 Figure 16: Framed submanifolds and a framed cobordism. Theorem C. Any compact framed submanifold .N; w/ of codimension p in M occurs as Pontryagin manifold for some smooth mapping f W M ! Sp . Thus the homopoty classes of maps are in one-one correspondence with the framed cobordism classes of submanifolds. The proof of Theorem A will be very similar to the argument in §§4 and 5. It will be based on three lemmas. Lemma 1. If v and v0 are two different positively oriented bases at y, then the Pontryagin manifold .f 1 .y/; f  v/ is framed cobordant to .f 1 .y/; f  v0 /. Proof. Choose a smooth path from v to v0 in the space of all positively oriented bases for T .Sp /y . This is possible since this space of bases can be identified with the space GLC .p; R/ of matrices with positive determinant, and hence is connected. Such a path gives rise to the required framing of the cobordism f 1 .y/  Œ0; 1. ■<br /> <br /> 44<br /> <br /> 7. Framed cobordism<br /> <br /> By abuse of notation we will often delete reference to f  v and speak simply of “the framed manifold f 1 .y/”. Lemma 2. If y is a regular value of f , and z is suﬀiciently close to y, then f 1 .z/ is framed cobordant to f 1 .y/. Proof. Since the set f .C / of critical values is compact, we can choose " > 0 so that the "-neighborhood of y contains only regular values. Given z with kz yk < ", choose a smooth one-parameter family of rotations (i.e. an isotopy) rt W Sp ! Sp so that r1 .y/ D z, and so that (1) rt is the identity for 0  t  "0 , (2) rt equals r 1 for 1 "0 < t  1, and (3) each rt 1 .z/ lies on the great circle from y to z, and hence is a regular value of f . Define the homotopy F W M  Œ0; 1 ! Sp by F .x; t / D rt f .x/. For each t note that z is a regular value of the composition rt ı f W M ! S p : It follows a fortiori that z is a regular value for the mapping F . Hence F<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .z/  M  Œ0; 1<br /> <br /> is a framed manifold and provides a framed cobordism between the framed manifolds f 1 .z/ and .rt ı f / 1 .z/ D f 1 ı rt 1 .z/ D f 1 .y/. This proves Lemma 2. ■ Lemma 3. If f and g are smoothly homotopic and y is a regular value for both, then f 1 .y/ is framed cobordant to g 1 .y/. Proof.<br /> <br /> Choose a homotopy F with ² f .x/; 0  t  "; F .x; t/ D g.x/; 1 " < t  1:<br /> <br /> Choose a regular