is compact, we could choose a sequence of such pairs with x converging, say to x0 , with x 0 converging to x00 , and with u ! 0 and u0 ! 0. Then clearly x0 D x00 , and we have contradicted the statement that g is one-one in a neighborhood of .x0 ; 0/. Thus g maps N  U" diffeomorphically onto an open set. But U" is diffeomorphic to the full Euclidean space Rp under the correspondence u 7!<br /> <br /> u 1<br /> <br /> kuk2 "<br /> <br /> :<br /> <br /> Since g.x; 0/ D x, and since dg.x;0/ does what is expected of it, this proves the Product Neighborhood Theorem for the special case M D RnCp . For the general case it is necessary to replace straightlines in RnCp by geodesics in M . More precisely let g.xI t1 ;    ; tp / be the endpoint of the geodesic segment of length kt1 v 1 .x/ C    C tp v p .x/k in M which starts at x with the initial velocity vector t1 v 1 .x/ C    C tp v p .x/ : kt1 v 1 .x/ C    C tp v p .x/k The reader who is familiar with geodesics will have no diﬀiculty in checking that gW N  U" ! M is well-defined and smooth, for " suﬀiciently small. The remainder of the proof proceeds exactly as before. ■ Proof of Theorem C. Let N  M be a compact, boundaryless, framed submanifold. Choose a product representation gW N  Rp ! V  M for a neighborhood V of N , as above, and define the projection W V ! Rp by .g.x; y// D y. (See Figure 18.) Clearly 0 is a regular value, and  is precisely N with its given framing.<br /> <br /> 1 .0/<br /> <br /> The Pontryagin construction<br /> <br /> v2<br /> <br /> 47<br /> <br /> Rp<br /> <br /> V  v1<br /> <br /> N Figure 18: Constructing a map with given Pontryagin manifold. Now choose a smooth map 'W Rp ! Sp which maps every x with kxk  1 into a base point s0 , and maps the open unit ball in Rp diffeomorphically1 onto Sp s0 . Define f W M ! Sp by f .x/ D<br /> <br /> ²<br /> <br /> '..x//; s0 ;<br /> <br /> x 2 V; x … V:<br /> <br /> Clearly f is smooth, and the point '.0/ is a regular value of f . Since the corresponding Pontryagin manifold f<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .'.0// D <br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .0/<br /> <br /> is precisely equal to the framed manifold N , this completes the proof of Theorem C. ■ In order to prove Theorem B we must first show that the Pontryagin manifold of a map determines its homopoty class. Let f; gW M ! Sp be smooth maps with a common regular value y. Lemma 4. If the framed manifold .f then f is smoothly homotopic to g.<br /> <br /> g  v/,<br /> <br /> 1 .y/; f  v/<br /> <br /> is equal to .g<br /> <br /> 1 .y/;<br /> <br /> Proof. It will be convenient to set N D f 1 .y/. The hypothesis that f  v D g  v means that dfx D dgx for all x 2 N . First suppose that f actually conicides with g throughout an entire neighborhood V of N . Let hW Sp y ! Rp be stereographic projection. Then the 1 For example, '.x/ D h 1 .x=.kxk2 //, where h is the stereographic projection from s 0 and where  is a smooth monotone decreasing function with .t/ > 0 for t < 1 and .t/ D 0 for t  1.<br /> <br /> 48<br /> <br /> 7. Framed cobordism<br /> <br /> homotopy H.x; t / D<br /> <br /> ²<br /> <br /> f .x/; h 1 Œt  h.f .x// C .1<br /> <br /> t /  h.g.x//;<br /> <br /> x 2 V; x2M<br /> <br /> N<br /> <br /> proves that f is smoothly homotopic to g. Thus is suﬀices‡‡ to deform f so that it coincides with g in some small neighborhood of N , being careful not to map any new points into y during the deformation. Choose a product representation N  Rp ! V  M for a neighborhood V of N , where V is small enough so that f .V / and g.V / do not contain the antipode yN of y. Identifying V with N  Rp and identifying Sp yN with Rp , we obtain corresponding mappings F; GW N  Rp ! Rp ; with F<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .0/ D G<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .0/ D N  0;<br /> <br /> and with dF.x;0/ D dG.x;0/ D .projection to Rp / for all x 2 N . We will first find a constant c so that F .x; u/  u > 0;<br /> <br /> G.x; u/  u > 0<br /> <br /> for x 2 N and 0 < kuk < c. That is, the points F .x; u/ and G.x; u/ belong to the same open half-space in Rp . So the homotopy .1<br /> <br /> t/F .x; u/ C tG.x; u/<br /> <br /> between F and G will not map any new points into 0, at least for kuk < c. By Taylor’s theorem kF .x; u/<br /> <br /> uk  c1 kuk2 ;<br /> <br /> for<br /> <br /> kuk  1:<br /> <br /> Hence j.F .x; u/<br /> <br /> u/  uj  c1 kuk3<br /> <br /> and F .x; u/  u  kuk2 ‡‡ Retypeset:<br /> <br /> c1 kuk3 > 0<br /> <br /> This is the original text. Maybe “it suﬀices...”<br /> <br /> The Pontryagin construction<br /> <br /> 49<br /> <br /> for 0 < kuk < c D min.c1 1 ; 1/, with a similar inequality for G. To avoid moving distant points we select a smooth map W Rp ! R with ² 1; kuk  c=2; .u/ D 0; kuk  c: Now the homotopy Ft .x; u/ D Œ1<br /> <br /> .u/tF .x; u/ C .u/tG.x; u/<br /> <br /> deforms F D F0 into a mapping F1 that (1) coincides with G in the region kuk < c=2, (2) coincides with F for kuk  c, and (3) has no new zeros. Making a corresponding deformation of the original mapping f , this clearly completes the proof of Lemma 4. ■ Proof of Theorem B. If f and g are smoothly homotopic, then Lemma 3 asserts that the Pontryagin manifolds f 1 .y/ and g 1 .y/ are framed cobordant. Conversely, given a framed cobordism .X; w/ between f 1 .y/ and g 1 .y/, an argument completely analogous to the proof of Theorem C constructs a homotopy F W M  Œ0; 1 ! Sp whose Pontryagin manifold .F 1 .y/; F  v/ is precisely equal to .X; w/. Setting Ft .x/ D F .x; t/, note that the maps F0 and f have exactly the same Pontryagin manifold. Hence F0  f by Lemma 4; and similarly F1  g. Therefore f  g, which completes the proof of Theorem B. ■<br /> <br /> Remark. Theorems A, B and C can easily be generalized so as to apply to a manifold M with boundary. The essential idea is to consider only mappings which carry the boundary into a base point s0 . The homotopy classes of such mappings .M; @M / ! .Sp ; s0 / are in one-one correspondence with the cobordism classes of framed submanifolds N  Interior.M /<br /> <br /> of codimension p. If p  12 m C 1, then this set of homotopy classes can be given the structure of an Abelian group, called the p-th cohomotopy group  p .M; @M /. The composition operation in  p .M; @M / corresponds to the union operation for disjoint framed submanifolds of Interior.M /. (Compare §8, Problem 17.)<br /> <br /> 50<br /> <br /> 7. Framed cobordism<br /> <br /> The Hopf theorem As an example, let M be a connected and oriented manifold of dimension m D p. A framed submanifold of codimension p is just a finite set of points with a preferred basis at each. Let sgn.x/ equal C1 or 1 according P as the preferred basis determines the right or wrong orientation. Then sgn.x/ is clearly equal to the degree of the associated map M ! Sm . But it is not diﬀicult to see that the framed P cobordism class of the 0-manifold is uniquely determined by this integer sgn.x/. Thus we have proved the following. Theorem of Hopf. If M is connected, oriented, and boundaryless, then two maps M ! Sm are smoothly homotopic if and only if they have the same degree. On the other hand, suppose that M is not orientable. Then given a basis for TMx we can slide x around M in a closed loop so as to tranform the given basis into one of opposite orientation. An easy argument the proves the following: Theorem. If M is connected but nonorientable, then two maps M ! Sm are homotopic if and only if they have the same mod 2 degree. The theory of framed cobordism was introduced by Pontryagin in order to study homotopy classes of mappings Sm ! Sp with m > p. For example if m D p C 1  4, there are precisely two homotopy classes of mappings Sm ! Sp . Pontryagin proved this result by classifying framed 1-manifolds in Sm . With considerably more diﬀiculty he was able to show that there are just two homotopy classes also in the case m D p C 2  4, using framed 2-manifolds. However, for m p > 2 this approach to the problem runs into manifold diﬀiculties. It has since turned out to be easier to enumerate homotopy classes of mappings by quite different, more algebraic methods2 . Pontryagin’s construction is, however, a double-edged tool. It not only allows us to translate information about manifolds into homotopy theory; it conversely enables us to translate any information about homotopy into manifold theory. Some of the deepest work in modern topology has come from the interplay of these two theories. Rene Thom’s work on cobordism is an important example of this. (References [36], [21].)<br /> <br /> 2 See<br /> <br /> for example S.-T. Hu, Homotopy Theory.<br /> <br /> 51<br /> <br /> 8 Exercises In conclusion here are some problems for the reader. Problem 1. Show that the degree of a composition g ı f is equal to the product .degree g/.degree f /. Problem 2. Show that every complex polynomial of degree n gives rise to a smooth map from the Gauss sphere S2 to itself of degree n. Problem 3. If two maps f and g from X to Sp satisfy kf .x/ g.x/k < 2 for all x, prove that f is homotopic to g, the homotopy being smooth if f and g are smooth. Problem 4. If X is compact, show that every continuous map X ! Sp can be uniformly approximated by a smooth map. If two smooth maps X ! Sp are continuously homotopic, show that they are smoothly homotopic. Problem 5. a constant.<br /> <br /> If m < p, show that every map M m ! Sp is homotopic to<br /> <br /> Problem 6 (Brouwer). Show that any map Sn ! Sn with degree different from . 1/nC1 must have a fixed point. Problem 7. Show that any map Sn ! Sn of odd degree must carry some pair of antipodal points into a pair of antipodal points. Problem 8. Given smooth manifolds M  Rk and N  Rl , show that the tangent sapce T .M  N /.x;y/ is equal to TMx  T Ny . Problem 9. The graph  of a smooth map f W M ! N is defined to be the set of all .x; y/ 2 M  N with f .x/ D y. Show that  is a smooth manifold and that the tangent space T .x;y/  TMx  T Ny is equal to the graph of the linear map dfx .<br /> <br /> 52<br /> <br /> 8. Exercises Problem 10.<br /> <br /> Given M  Rk , show that the tangent bundle space TM D ¹ .x; v/ 2 M  Rk j v 2 TMx º<br /> <br /> is also a smooth manifold. Show that any smooth map f W M ! N gives rise to a smooth map df W TM ! T N where d.identity/ D identity; Problem 11.<br /> <br /> d.g ı f / D .dg/ ı .df /:<br /> <br /> Similarly show that the normal bundle space E D ¹ .x; v/ 2 M  Rk j v ? TMx º<br /> <br /> is a smooth manifold. If M is compact and boundaryless, show that the correspondence .x; v/ 7! x C v<br /> <br /> from E to Rk maps the "-neighborhood of M  0 in E diffeomorphically onto the "-neighborhood N" of M in Rk . (Compare the Product Neighborhood Theorem in §7.) Problem 12. Define rW N" ! M by r.x C v/ D x. Show that r.x C v/ is closer to x C v than any other point of M . Using this retraction r, prove the analogue of Problem 4 in which the sphere Sp is replaced by a manifold M . Problem 13.<br /> <br /> Given disjoint manifolds M; N  RkC1 , the linking map W M  N ! Sk<br /> <br /> is defined by .x; y/ D kxx yyk . If M and N are compact, oriented, and boundaryless, with total dimension m C n D k, then the degree of  is called the linking number l.M; N /. Prove that l.N; M / D . 1/.mC1/.nC1/ l.M; N /: If M bounds an oriented manifold X disjoint from N , prove that l.M; N / D 0. Define the linking number for disjoint manifolds in the sphere SmCnC1 . Problem 14 (The Hopf Invariant). If y ¤ z are regular values for a map f W S2p 1 ! Sp , then the manifolds f 1 .y/, f 1 .z/ can be oriented as in §5; hence the linking number l.f 1 .y/; f 1 .z// is defined. a) Prove that this linking number is locally constant as a function of y.<br /> <br /> Exercises<br /> <br /> 53<br /> <br /> b) If y and z are regular values of g also, where kf .x/<br /> <br /> g.x/k < ky<br /> <br /> zk<br /> <br /> for all x, prove that l f<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .y/; f<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br />  .z/ D l g<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .y/; f<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br />  .z/ D l g<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .y/; g<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br />  .z/ :<br /> <br /> c) Prove that l.f 1 .y/; f 1 .z// depends only on the homotopy class of f , and does not depend on the choice of y and z. This integer H.f / D l.f 1 .y/; f 1 .z// is called the Hopf invariant of f . (Reference [15].) Problem 15. composition<br /> <br /> If the dimension p is odd, prove that H.f / D 0. For a S2p<br /> <br /> 1 f<br /> <br /> g<br /> <br /> ! Sp ! Sp<br /> <br /> prove that H.g ı f / is equal to H.f / multiplied by the square of the degree of g. The Hopf fibration W S3 ! S2 is defined by   x1 C ix2 .x1 ; x2 ; x3 ; x4 / D h 1 x3 C ix4 where h denotes stereographic projection to the complex plane. Prove that H./ D 1. Problem 16. Two submanifolds N and N 0 of M are said to intersect transversally if, for each x 2 N \ N 0 , the subspaces T Nx and T Nx0 together generate TMx . (If n C n0 < m this means that N \ N 0 D ∅.) If N is a framed submanifold, prove that it can be deformed slightly so as to intersect a given N 0 transversally. Prove that the resulting intersection is a smooth manifold. Problem 17. Let …p .M / denote the set of all framed cobordism classes of codimension p in M . Use the transverse intersection operation to define a correspondence …p .M /  …q ! …pCq .M /:<br /> <br /> If p  12 m C 1, use the disjoint union operation to make …p .M / into an Abelian group. (Compare p. 49.)<br /> <br /> 54<br /> <br /> A<br /> <br /> Classifying 1-manifolds<br /> <br /> We will prove the following result, which has been assumed in the text. A brief discussion of the classification problem for higher dimensional manifolds will also be given. Theorem. Any smooth, connected 1-dimensional manifold is diffeomorphic either to the circle S1 or to some interval of real numbers. (An interval is a connected subset of R which is not a poiint. It may be finite or infinite; closed, open, or half-open.) Since any interval is diffeomorphic1 either to Œ0; 1, .0; 1, or .0; 1/, it follows that there are only four distinct connected 1-manifolds. The proof will make use of the concept of “arc-length”. Let I be an interval. Definition. A map f W I ! M is a parametrization by arc-length if f maps I diffeomorphically onto an open subset2 of M , and if the “velocity vector” dfs .1/ 2 TMf .s/ has unit length, for each s 2 I . Any given local parametrization I 0 ! M can be transformed into a parametrization by arc-length by straightforward change of variables. Lemma 1. Let f W I ! M and gW J ! M be parametrizations by arclength. The f .I / \ g.J / has at most two components. If it has only one component, then f can be extended to a parametrization by arc-length of the union f .I / [ g.J /. If it has two conponents, then M must be diffeomorphic to S1 . Proof. Clearly g 1 ı f maps some relatively open subset of I diffeomorphically onto a relatively open subset of J . Furthermore the derivative of g 1 ı f is equal to ˙1 everywhere. Consider the graph   I  J , consisting of all .s; t / with f .s/ D g.t /. Then  is a closed subset of I  J made up of line segments of slope ˙1. 1 For<br /> <br /> example, use a diffeomorphism of the form f .t/ D a tanh.t/ C b:<br /> <br /> 2 Thus<br /> <br /> I can have boundary points only if M has boundary points.<br /> <br /> 55 Since  is closed and g 1 ı f is locally a diffeomorphism, there line segments cannot end in the interior of I  J , but must extend to the boundary. Since g 1 ı f is one-one and single valued, there can be at most one of the segments ending on each of the four edges of the rectangle I  J . Hence  has at most two components. (See Figure 19.) Furthermore, if there are two components, the two must have the same slope. ˇ ˛<br /> <br /> or<br /> <br /> or<br /> <br /> ı<br /> <br /> a<br /> <br /> b<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> c d<br /> <br /> Figure 19: Three of the possibilities for . If  is connected, then g 1 ı f extends to a linear map LW R ! R. Now f and g ı L piece together to yield the required extension FWI [ L<br /> <br /> 1<br /> <br /> .J / ! f .I / [ g.J /:<br /> <br /> If  has two components, with slope say C1, they must be arranged as in the left-hand rectangle of Figure 19. Translating the interval J D . ; ˇ/ if necessary, we may assume that D c and ı D d , so that a < b  c < d  ˛ < bet a: Now setting  D<br /> <br /> 2 t ˛ a,<br /> <br /> the required diffeomorphism hW S1 ! M<br /> <br /> is defined by the formula h.cos ; sin  / D<br /> <br /> ²<br /> <br /> f .t /; g.t /;<br /> <br /> a < t < d; c < t < ˇ:<br /> <br /> The image h.S1 /, being compact and open in M , must be the entire manifold M . This proves the lemma. ■ Proof of Classification Theorem Any parametrization by arc-length can be extended to one f WI ! M which is maximal in the sense that f cannot be extended over any larger interval<br /> <br /> 56<br /> <br /> A. Classifying 1-manifolds<br /> <br /> as a parametrization by arc-length: it is only necessary to extend f as far as possible to the left and then as far as possible to the right. If M is not diffeomorphic to S1 , we will prove that f in onto, and hence is a diffeomorphism. For if the open set f .I / were not all of M , there would be a limit point x of f .I / in M f .I /. Parametrizing a neighborhood of x by arc-length and applying the lemma, we would see that f can be extended over a larger interval. This contradicts the assumption that f is maximal and hence completes the proof. ■ Remark. For manifolds of higher dimension the classification problem becomes quite formidable. For 2-dimensional manifold, a thorough exposition has been given by Kerekjarto [17]. The study of 3-dimensional manifolds is very much a topic of current research. (See Papakyriakopoulos [26].) For compact manifolds of dimension  4 the classification problem is actually unsolvable3 . But for high dimensional simply connected manifolds there has been much progress in recent years, as exemplified by the work of Smale [31] and Wall [37].<br /> <br /> 3 See<br /> <br /> Markov [19].<br /> <br /> 57<br /> <br /> Bibliography∗ [1] Allendoerfer, C. B., “The Euler number of a Riemann manifold”, Amer. Jour. Math. 62 (1940), 243-248. [2] Apostol, T. M., Mathematical Analysis. Reading, Mass.: AddisonWesley, 1957. [3] Auslander, L. and R. Mackenzie, Introduction to Differentiable Manifolds. New York: McGraw-Hill, 1963. [4] Brouwer, L. E. J., “Uber Abbildung von Mannigfaltigkeiten”, Math. Annalen 71 (1912), 97-115. [5] Brown, A. B., “Functional dependence”, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 38 (1935), 379-394. (See Theorem 3-III.) [6] Chern, S. S., “A simple intrinsic proof of the Gauss-Bonnet formula for closed Riemannian manifolds”, Annals of Math. 45 (1944), 747-752. [7] Dieudonne, J., Foundations of Modern Analysis. New York: Academic Press, 1960. [8] Dubovickiǐ, A. Ya., “On differentiable mappings of an n-dimensional cube into a k-dimensional cube”, Mat. Sbornik N.S. 32 (74), (1953), 443464. (In Russian.) [9] Fenchel, W., “On total curvature of Riemannian manifolds”, Jour. London Math. Soc. 15 (1940), 15-22. [10] Goffman, C., Calculus of Several Variables. New York: Harper & Row, 1965. [11] Hilton, P., and S. Wylie, Homology Theory. Cambridge Univ. Press, 1960. [12] Hirsch, M., “A proof of the nonretractibility of a cell onto its boundary”, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 14 (1963), 364-365. ∗ The<br /> <br /> following is a miscellaneous list consisting of original sources and of recommended textbooks. For the reader who wishes to pursue the study of differential topology, let me recommend Milnor [22], Munkres [25], and Pontryagin [28]. The survey articles [23] and [32] should also prove useful. For background knowledge in closely related fields, let me recommend Hilton and Wylie [11], Hu [16], Lang [18], de Rham [29], Steenrod [34] and Sternberg [35].<br /> <br /> 58<br /> <br /> Bibliography∗<br /> <br /> [13] Hopf, H., “Abbildungsklassen n-dimensionaler Mannigfaltigkeiten”, Math. Annalen 96 (1926), 209-224. [14] Hopf, H, “Vektorfelder in n-dimensionalen Mannigfaltigkeiten”, Math. Annalen 96 (1926) 225-250. [15] Hopf, H. “Uber die Abbildungen von Spharen auf Spharen niedrigerer Dimension”, Fundamenta Mathematicae 25 (1935), 427-440. [16] Hu, S.-T., Homotopy Theory. New York: Academic Press, 1959. [17] Kerekjarto, B. v., Vorlesungen über Topologie. Berlin: Springer, 1923. [18] Lang, S., Introduction to Differential Manifolds. New York: Interscience, 1962. [19] Markov, A. A., “Insolubility of the problem of homeomorphy”, Proceedings Intern. Congress of Math. 1958, Cambridge Univ. Press, 1960, pp. 300-306. (In Russian.) [20] Milnor, J., “On manifolds homeomorphic to the 7-sphere”, Annals of Math. 64 (1956), 399-405. [21] Milnor, J., “A survey of cobordism theory”, L’Enseignement math. 8 (1962), 16-23. [22] Milnor, J., Morse Theory. (Annals Studies 51.) Princeton Univ. Press, 1963. [23] Milnor, J., “Diffrential topology”, Lectures on Modern Mathematics, II, ed. T. L. Saaty, New York: Wiley, 1964, pp. 165-183. [24] Morse, A. P., “The behavior of a function on its critical set”, Annals of Math. 40 (1939), 62-70. [25] Munkres, J. R., Elementary Differential Topology. (Annals Studies 54). Princeton Univ. Press, 1963. [26] Papakyriakopoulos, C. D., “The theory of three-dimensional manifolds since 1950”, Proceedings Intern. Congress of Math. 1958, Cambridge Univ. Press, 1960, pp.433-440. [27] Pontryagin, L. S., “A classification of continuous transformations of a complex into a sphere”, Doklady Akad. Nark. S.S.S.R. (Comptes Rendues) 19 (1938), 147-149.<br /> <br /> 59 [28] Pontryagin, L. S., “Smooth manifolds and their applications in homotopy theory”, Amer. Math. Soc. Translations, Ser. 2, II (1959), 1-114. (Translated from Trudy Inst. Steklov 45 (1955).) [29] Rham, G. de, Varietes differentiables, Paris: Hermann, 1955. [30] Sard, A., “The measure of critical points of differentiable maps”, Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. 48 (1942), 883-890. [31] Smale, S., “Generalized Poincaré’s conjecture in dimensions greater than four”, Annals of Math. 74 (1961), 391-406. [32] Smale, S., “A survey of some recent developments in differential topology”, Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. 69 (1963), 131-145. [33] Spivak, M., Calculus on Manifolds. New York: Benjamin, 1965. [34] Steenrod, N., The Topology of Fibre Bundles. Princeton Univ. Press, 1951. [35] Sternberg, S., Lectures on Differential Geometry. New York: PrenticeHall, 1964. [36] Thom, R., “Quelques proprietes globales des varietes differentiables”, Commentarii Math. Helvet. 28 (1954), 17-86. [37] Wall, C. T. C., “Classification of .n 1/-connected 2n-manifolds”, Annals of Math. 75 (1962), 163-189. [38] Whitney, H., “A function not constant on a connected set of critical points”, Duke Math. Jour. 1 (1935), 514-517. Added April, 1969: [39] Husemoller, D., Fiber Bundles. New York: McGraw-Hill, 1966. [40] Spanier, E. H., Algebraic Topology. New York: McGraw-Hill, 1966. [41] Wall, C. T. C., “Topology of smooth manifolds”, J. London Math. Soc. 40 (1965), 1-20. [42] Wallace, A. H., Differential Topology, First Steps. New York: Benjamin, 1968.<br /> <br /> 60<br /> <br /> Index‡‡ deg2 .f /, 24 mod 2 degree, 24<br /> <br /> Fubini theorem, 17 fundamental theorem of algebra, 8<br /> <br /> A. B. Brown, 10<br /> <br /> Gauss mapping, 35 geodesic, 46 graph, 51, 54<br /> <br /> boundary, 12 Brouwer fixed point theorem, 13 Brouwer, L. E. J., 13, 27, 30, 51 chain rule, 3, 7 cobordant, 41 cobordism, 41 codimension, 41 cohomotopy group, 49 coordinates, 1 correspond, 32 critical point, 8, 10 critical value, 8, 10 curvatura integra, 36 degree, 27 derivative, 2, 3, 6 diffeomorphism, 1 differential topology, 1 dimension, 1 disk, 12<br /> <br /> half-space, 12 Hirsch, M., 13 homogeneity lemma, 22 homotopy, 20 homotopy lemma, 21 Hopf fibration, 53 Hopf invariant, 53 Hopf, H., 31, 35, 50, 52 index, 32, 33 interior, 12 interval, 54 inverse function theorem, 4 isotopic, 21, 23 linking number, 52 manifold with boundary, 12 measurable set, 17 Morse, A. P., 10 Morse, M., 40<br /> <br /> Euclidean length, 14 Euclidean space, 1 Euler number, 35<br /> <br /> nondegenerate, 36 normal bundle space, 52 normal vector, 11<br /> <br /> fixed point, 13, 14 framed cobordant, 41 framed submanifold, 41 framing, 41<br /> <br /> opposite orientation, 26 orientation, 26 orientation preserving, 26 oriented smooth manifold, 26<br /> <br /> ‡‡ Retypeset:<br /> <br /> This section may be somewhat different from the original text.<br /> <br /> 61 parametrization, 1 parametrization by arc-length, 54 Poincaré, H., 35 Poincaré-Hopf theorem, 35 Pontryagin manifold, 42 Pontryagin, L., 16, 41, 50 product neighborhood theorem, 45 regular point, 7 regular value, 7, 10 residue class, 20 same orientation, 26 Sard, A., 10, 16 sign, 27 smooth, 1 smooth m-manifold with boundary, 12<br /> <br /> smooth homotopy, 20 smooth manifold, 1 smoothly homotopic, 20 smoothly isotopic, 21 space of normal vectors, 11 sphere, 2 standard orientation, 26 stereographic projection, 8 tangent bundle space, 52 tangent space, 2–4, 12 tangent vector, 2 tangent vector field, 30 Taylor’s theorem, 19 Thom, R., 50 transverse, 53 vector field, 30<br /> <br /> 62<br /> <br /> Index of Symbols‡‡ TMx , 2 Rk , 1 Dm , 12 Hm , 12 Sn 1 , 2 deg.f I y/, 27 dfx , 2<br /> <br /> ‡‡ Retypeset:<br /> <br /> kxk, 14 @X, 12 ]f 1 .y/, 8 f  g, 20 f  v, 42 gıf,1<br /> <br /> This section may be somewhat different from the original text.</p>
